      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This \"vanishing\" behavior in random patches suggests the color wasn't fully cured before the heat press was exposed to an atmosphere that triggers rapid migration or alkalinity damage. Sublimation ink can diffuse into internal binder pockets if the fabric's surface tension isn't perfectly controlled after curing, allowing pigment to spread away from the press zone face when heated unevenly. You might suspect a breakdown in your binding agent chemistry interacting with ambient air moisture or a localized issue in the heating element causing hot and cold spots that drive this pigment diffusion.",
